<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edo State is a state in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1991 from the former Bendel State, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> as its capital. It covers the heartland of the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a> and the lands of the Esan, Owan, Etsako and other peoples. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-benin">Oba of Benin</a> and the Esan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Enijie</a> remain traditional rulers in the state with ceremonial and religious authority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-state.jpg" alt="Edo State" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hadassah Photostorie group · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_hill_view,_Ososo,_Akoko-Edo-8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Edo State lies in the South-South geopolitical zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a> to the north, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> to the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anambra-state">Anambra State</a> to the east, Delta State to the south-east and south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ondo-state">Ondo State</a> to the west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> is its capital. The state has a tropical wet and dry climate, and its resources include crude oil, limestone and other minerals.</p><p>Its modern borders encompass regions that were formerly the site of various empires and kingdoms, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, formed in the 11th century. The ancient city of Edo, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, was home to some of the largest earthworks in the world. In 1897 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> conducted a punitive expedition and invasion, destroying most of the ancient city of Edo and incorporating the territory into what became the Southern Nigeria Protectorate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Mid-Western Region came into being in June 1963, formed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and Delta provinces of the Western Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> as its capital. When a twelve-state structure was introduced in 1967 it became Midwest State, and in 1976 the territory was renamed Bendel State. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a>, Biafran forces negotiated a passage through the Mid-Western state on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, and under Biafran occupation the state was declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin">Republic of Benin</a>, which collapsed a day after the declaration as Nigerian troops took Benin City. Edo State was established on 27 August 1991, when Bendel State was split into Edo and Delta States.</p><p>The traditional political organisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo people</a> rests on a three-tier age-group system that makes age rather than lineage the criterion of authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> itself is organised by age-grades, by wards and by chiefs, hereditary and non-hereditary, who serve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> in specialised functions. The Oba is the focal point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> political system, which rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The state is inhabited mainly by Edoid peoples, including the Edo or Bini, Esan, Owan, Afemai (Etsako and Akoko-Edo) and Ora, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> and others. Edo-speaking people make up the largest share, and the Edo language is commonly spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, while English is the official language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was introduced by Portuguese missionaries in the 15th century and is dominant in the south; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is dominant in the north, and traditional religions are also practised. The state consists of eighteen local government areas and has about five million inhabitants. Benin City is the centre of the country's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edo State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They became part of Southern Nigeria, and the Enijie remain traditional rulers in <b>Edo State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/esan-chiefdoms">Esan chiefdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nigerian independence the Oba remained a traditional ruler, today in <b>Edo State</b>, with ceremonial and religious authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-benin-restored">Kingdom of Benin (restored)</a></li></ul></section>
